## Decoding the question
**"Critically evaluate"** asks you to judge a concept: explain **gendered globalisation**, use **world-system theory** as the lens (it is named), show **how** global capitalism has created **new forms of patriarchal power**, and then **weigh** the claim that these increase gender inequality against evidence that globalisation also empowers some women. Name the feminist scholars who gendered world-system theory.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Globalisation** is the integration of economies and societies through trade, investment, finance, technology and migration. **Gendered globalisation** is the idea that this integration **relies on, reshapes and often deepens** gender inequality — while also creating some new opportunities for women.

#### ii) The argument
**World-system theory**, once extended by feminists, shows that global capitalism depends on women's **cheap and unpaid labour** at the periphery. Globalisation has produced **new forms of patriarchal power** — in factories, labour markets, migration, debt and culture. Yet the evidence also shows gains for some women. The claim is therefore **largely true but incomplete**.

### 2. Gendered globalisation defined
Globalisation is "gendered" in three senses: it **uses** existing gender inequalities (women's lower wages and unpaid care) to cut costs; it **reshapes** gender relations (drawing women into paid work, sending men and women abroad); and it **creates** new inequalities and new forms of control. Economists and sociologists such as **Joan Acker** and **Saskia Sassen** have analysed globalisation in these terms.

### 3. World-system theory
**Immanuel Wallerstein** (*The Modern World-System*, ==1974==) argued that since the sixteenth century there has been one **capitalist world-economy** divided into the **core** (rich, industrial, controlling finance and technology), the **periphery** (poor, supplying raw materials and cheap labour) and the **semi-periphery** in between. Surplus flows from periphery to core through **unequal exchange**.

### 4. The gender blind spot and feminist extensions

#### i) The blind spot
Classic world-system theory spoke of states and classes, not women and men. It explained why the periphery was poor but not why, within the periphery, women were poorer than men and did most of the unpaid work.

#### ii) Households
**Joan Smith and Immanuel Wallerstein** (*Creating and Transforming Households*, ==1992==) showed that households survive by combining wages with **unpaid work, subsistence farming and petty trade** — allowing employers to pay **below a living wage**. The unpaid labour that fills the gap is mostly women's — cooking, caring, growing food and making things for sale.

#### iii) Housewifisation
**Maria Mies** (==1986==) argued that the world-system rests on the exploitation of **nature, colonies and women**, whose work is defined as "housework" and cheapened.

#### iv) The global assembly line
**Annette Fuentes and Barbara Ehrenreich** (*Women in the Global Factory*, ==1983==) and **Kathryn Ward** (==1990==) documented how firms moved production to the periphery and hired young women.

::: fig pyramid | The gendered world-system.
Core: finance, design, brands and profits
Semi-periphery: industrial production and some rising middle classes
Periphery: raw materials, cheap and unpaid female labour
:::

### 5. New patriarchal power (1): the workplace
In export factories, **corporate managers** control women workers through low wages, long hours, production targets, surveillance and **harassment** — a **workplace patriarchy** added to the family's. Unions are often discouraged in export processing zones, and young women are preferred precisely because they are seen as less likely to organise. Control that once belonged to fathers and husbands is now shared with supervisors and global brands.

### 6. New patriarchal power (2): feminised, flexible labour
**Guy Standing** (==1989==) described the **feminisation of labour**: jobs become flexible, informal and low-paid, and are increasingly filled by women. Global brands subcontract to **home-based workers** who have no contracts or social security. The home becomes a workplace where women earn piece rates while doing all the housework — patriarchy and capital meeting in the same room.

### 7. New patriarchal power (3): global care chains
**Arlie Hochschild** (==2000==) described **global care chains**: women from the periphery care for families in the core while their own children are cared for by relatives. Under sponsorship (*kafala*) systems, migrant domestic workers face confiscated passports, unpaid wages and abuse — control exercised by employers and states.

### 8. New patriarchal power (4): debt and austerity
Debt to core-controlled lenders brings **austerity**. **Diane Elson** (==1991==) showed that cuts treat women's unpaid labour as "infinitely elastic", shifting the costs of adjustment onto women.

### 9. New patriarchal power (5): bodies and backlash

#### i) Commodification
Global industries commodify women's bodies — **trafficking**, commercial surrogacy, and beauty industries selling skin-lightening products.

#### ii) Backlash
Rapid change can provoke **cultural backlash**: local patriarchs tighten control over women's dress, mobility and work in the name of protecting "our culture" from globalisation.

### 10. Evidence that inequality has increased
The **Rana Plaza** collapse in Bangladesh (==24 April 2013==) killed more than 1,100 garment workers, most of them women; the **Ali Enterprises** fire in Karachi (==2012==) killed about 260. Women remain concentrated in the lowest-paid segments of global value chains. The World Economic Forum estimated in ==2026== that the global gender gap was only ==69.2%== closed, with parity ==120 years== away.

### 11. Counter-evidence
Yet globalisation also **opened doors**. Export jobs gave millions of young women in Bangladesh and elsewhere their **first independent income**. **Naila Kabeer** (*The Power to Choose*, ==2000==) found that garment work delayed marriage, increased women's say at home and was valued by the women themselves. Global norms (**CEDAW**, **SDG 5**) and trade conditions give activists leverage, and pressure after Rana Plaza produced the **Bangladesh Accord** on building safety (==2013==). World-system theory, by treating women as victims, can miss this **agency** — women workers' unions, cross-border campaigns and fair-trade networks.

::: fig compare | Evaluating gendered globalisation.
## New patriarchal power
- Workplace control and harassment
- Feminised, informal, insecure work
- Care drained through global chains
- Austerity shifts costs to women
- Commodified bodies; backlash
## Countervailing gains
- First wages and independence
- Delayed marriage; bargaining power
- Global rights norms and leverage
- Campaigns for safety and rights
- Women organising across borders
:::

### 12. The Pakistan angle
Pakistan sits in the **periphery or lower semi-periphery**, exporting **textiles, cotton and rice** and labour. Women are concentrated at the bottom of textile chains as **home-based** stitchers and cotton pickers; **Sindh**'s **Home-Based Workers Act 2018** began to protect them. Men's migration brought record remittances — more than 38 billion dollars in 2024–25 — leaving many women running households. The EU's **GSP+** (from ==2014==) links trade access to **CEDAW** and labour standards. Female labour-force participation remains ==24.4%==.

### 13. Conclusion
Gendered globalisation is a powerful concept. Seen through a **feminist world-system lens**, global capitalism depends on women's cheap and unpaid labour and has created **new forms of patriarchal power** — in factories, informal chains, care migration, austerity and culture — that deepen inequality. But the claim is **incomplete**: globalisation has also given many women wages, voice and rights language. Whether it increases or reduces inequality depends on **labour rights, social protection, state policy and women's own organising** across borders.

## Why this answer scores
- Defines **gendered globalisation** and uses **world-system theory** as instructed, with its **feminist extensions**.
- Identifies **five new forms of patriarchal power**, each with evidence.
- **Critically evaluates** with counter-evidence (Kabeer, the Bangladesh Accord).
- A **pyramid** and a **compare figure**, plus Pakistan, support a reasoned verdict.
